sh: enable -fsection-anchors

The SH backend did not declare an anchor offset range, so
-fsection-anchors was ignored ("this target does not support
-fsection-anchors").  Declare 0..60, the reach of mov.l @(disp,Rn).

With -fsection-anchors, globals of one section are placed at known
offsets from an anchor: a function loads one base address from the
literal pool and reaches the others with @(disp,Rn), instead of one
pool entry and one address load per global.

Measured on sh-elf -m2 (GCC 14.3, GDB SH sim): a game-style program
with about 30 scalar globals is 15% smaller (.text) and 2% faster at
-O2; the torture execute suite is 1.4% smaller overall; zlib and
array-based code are unchanged.  No effect with -fdata-sections, which
puts every variable in its own section.  Torture execute results are
unchanged at -O1/-O2/-O3/-Os with and without the option.  Without
-fsection-anchors the generated code is unchanged.

--- a/gcc-12.4.0/gcc/config/sh/sh.cc
+++ b/gcc-12.4.0/gcc/config/sh/sh.cc
@@ -380,6 +380,12 @@
 #undef TARGET_ASM_FUNCTION_EPILOGUE
 #define TARGET_ASM_FUNCTION_EPILOGUE sh_output_function_epilogue
 
+/* Anchors for -fsection-anchors: mov.l @(disp,Rn) reaches 60 bytes.  */
+#undef TARGET_MIN_ANCHOR_OFFSET
+#define TARGET_MIN_ANCHOR_OFFSET 0
+#undef TARGET_MAX_ANCHOR_OFFSET
+#define TARGET_MAX_ANCHOR_OFFSET 60
+
 #undef TARGET_ASM_OUTPUT_MI_THUNK
 #define TARGET_ASM_OUTPUT_MI_THUNK sh_output_mi_thunk
 
